      "32": "13.2 The exact refinement substitution\nThe refinement step is worth displaying as a substitution table. It is the arithmetic bridge between the\nexplicit matrix recursion and a maximal field.\nPublished requirement\nValue in the recursive construction\nInitial simple stratum\n[A, n, R, γ]\nPerturbation\nb = LiftE(C) ∈P−R\nDerived parameter\ns(b) = C\nPure maximal derived field\nE[C] ⊆Mpd−1(E) by induction\nOuter critical exponent\nk0(γ, A) = −n\nInner critical exponent\nk0(C, B) = −q1\nStrict inequality\n−2R > −n −q1\nLiteral output field\nK = F[γ + b]\nFor odd p, n > pR > 2R. For p = 2, n > 2R. Both imply the required strict inequality. Maximality\nof the derived field is a genuine induction hypothesis. It is not implied merely by counting its matrix\nentries.\n13.3 Inputs that remain separately audited or deferred\nThe marked source attachment remains a scalar-pushout contract. Transporting the whole source mark-\ning does not identify it with an independently fixed host marking. The full finite-image center in low\nrank can exceed order two. Finally the native stabilizer obstruction and the same-candidate factor re-\nquirement are unchanged. These boundaries remain visible even though the parameter, character, and\nenlarged-induction arguments are now developed at greater length.\nThe conductor formulas in Sections 6–8 retain their pinned arithmetic sources. Displayed numerical\ntheorem. The source-defined Bushnell–Henniart induction and descent branch remains separate from\nthe direct matrix constructor and cannot serve as an unstated existence step in its proof.\n13.4 Distinguishing evidence from completion\nspecializations are checked where indicated. This is not an end-to-end audit of every conductor\nWritten proofs, finite tests and formal declarations have distinct scopes. None supplies a missing\narithmetic input.\nRealization Calculus\nFURTHER STUDIES\n1512\n",
      "33": "The two matrix scripts use sparse Laurent arithmetic in positive characteristic. They check actual re-\nB Finite checks and their limits\nThe recorded replay covers four bounded gates. The initial evidence suite (Block 01) passed twelve\ntest methods. The direct-construction suite passed eleven methods covering uniformizer identities,\nnative and amplified traces, correction-depth endpoints, character extension, determinant transfer,\nlow-rank commutators, filtration inequalities, and noncompact recovery. The exact rank-nine odd and\nrank-eight dyadic matrix scripts also passed unchanged in isolated working directories.\nThe direct-construction suite includes 256 binary leading-commutator cases, 153 low-rank\nnormalization parameter pairs, 2,211 finite cyclic character-extension cases, and 972 finite\nLaurent-kernel cases. These are distinct finite model families, not a count of universal theorems. Other\nbounds and negative controls are listed separately in the JSON receipts. No aggregate assertion count\nis used as a measure of progress toward Conjecture 66.\ncursive matrix identities and grades, not merely floating-point approximations. They do not prove field\nmaximality, simple-character existence, source attachment, the H5 factor comparison, or a universal\nrealization statement. A proof reconstruction in prose is likewise not a Lean compilation.\nArithmetic realization and its obstructions\nFURTHER STUDIES\n1513\n",
      "34": "The arithmetic proof route is complemented by the monomial, Clifford-descent, and binder analysis in\nSections 14–21, the conductor and marking analysis in Sections 22–30, and the research atlas. These\nan exhaustive reconciliation of every archived construction.\ndevelopments preserve the separate source and verification limits of each argument. They do not claim\nRealization Calculus\nFURTHER STUDIES\n1514\n"
